{#- The Main navigation bar. Included by base.njk on every page, and by
    content/unstrung/app/index.njk on the web app's page, which has none of the
    site's other styles: everything the bar needs is in assets/css/menu.css and
    assets/js/menu.js.

    Every link has tabindex="0". Safari on a Mac leaves links out of the tab
    order unless its "Press Tab to highlight each item" setting is on, which
    left only the two dropdown buttons reachable by Tab, and no item inside a
    dropdown. An explicit tabindex puts a link in the order whatever that
    setting says. Other browsers already stop on links, so nothing changes
    there. base.njk does the same for the skip link and the banner. -#}
    <nav class="main-menu" aria-label="Main">
      <ul role="list">
        <li><a href="/" tabindex="0"{% if page.url == "/" %} aria-current="page"{% endif %}>Home</a></li>
        <li>
          <details class="submenu">
            <summary>Articles</summary>
            <ul role="list">
              <li><a href="/articles/" tabindex="0"{% if page.url == "/articles/" %} aria-current="page"{% endif %}>All articles</a></li>
              {%- for article in collections.articles %}
              <li><a href="{{ article.url }}" tabindex="0"{% if page.url == article.url %} aria-current="page"{% endif %}>{{ article.data.title }}</a></li>
              {%- endfor %}
            </ul>
          </details>
        </li>
        <li>
          <details class="submenu">
            <summary>unstrung</summary>
            <ul role="list">
              {%- for item in [
                { url: "/unstrung/", label: "Overview" },
                { url: "/unstrung/app/", label: "The app" },
                { url: "/unstrung/docs/", label: "Documentation" },
                { url: "/unstrung/videos/", label: "Videos" }
              ] %}
              <li><a href="{{ item.url }}" tabindex="0"{% if page.url == item.url %} aria-current="page"{% endif %}>{{ item.label }}</a></li>
              {%- endfor %}
            </ul>
          </details>
        </li>
        {#- Read as "About eyesunstrung", so arrowing past the end of the
            unstrung dropdown does not sound like About unstrung. The visible
            text starts the name, as WCAG 2.5.3 asks. #}
        <li><a href="/about/" tabindex="0" aria-label="About eyesunstrung"{% if page.url == "/about/" %} aria-current="page"{% endif %}>About</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>
